+# Case-Insensitive Matching Folds Case in the Query, Not the Column
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-003` narrows the veterinarian directory to one specialty, matched whole and regardless of letter case. The match must behave alike on H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.
+
+The three vendor schemas do not agree on case. The specialty name column is case-sensitive under H2 and PostgreSQL. MySQL's default collation is case-insensitive. Owner search already depends on the column, and it is case-sensitive under PostgreSQL alone, a recorded defect against `REQ-OWN-002`. The H2-backed default suite could not see that divergence.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Fold case in the schema.** Retype the column per vendor: an ignore-case type under H2, a case-insensitive type or collation under PostgreSQL, the default collation under MySQL. This means three hand-maintained scripts with three different mechanisms, and the H2 suite proves only one of them.
+2. **Fold case in the application.** Lower-case the parameter in Java and compare it to a column that is already lower-case. That holds only while every stored name stays lower-case, and nothing enforces it.
+3. **Fold case in the query** (chosen). The query applies the same case function to the column and to the parameter, inside one equality.
+
+## Decision
+
+A read that matches a name regardless of letter case folds case on both operands inside the query. Its case-insensitivity never depends on a column's type or collation. One database engine then folds both sides, so the comparison holds on every supported vendor and the H2 suite exercises the same logic.
+
+The match is an equality, never a pattern match. A prefix or a wildcard character in the caller's value therefore matches nothing.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The specialty narrowing needs no schema change on any vendor.
+- An index over the raw column does not serve the folded comparison. The specialty table is a short lookup list, so this costs nothing measurable here; a large table would need an expression index per vendor.
+- Case folding of non-ASCII letters is each engine's own. MySQL's default collation still governs the comparison, so accent differences are ignored there as well. The seeded specialty names are unaccented ASCII.
+- Owner search does not conform. It relies on the column, which is why it breaks under PostgreSQL. A repair of that defect meets this decision.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/VetRepository.java`: the narrowed read.
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence)
+- [Script-Managed Schema Across Three Database Vendors](2026-07-31-script-managed-multi-vendor-schema.md) — why the vendor schemas differ
